An **iterator** produces a sequence of items, one at a time, and knows when it is finished. Iterators in Rust are **lazy**: creating one does nothing until you consume it.

```rust
let v1 = vec![1, 2, 3];
let v1_iter = v1.iter(); // nothing happens yet

for val in v1_iter {     // the for loop consumes it
    println!("Got: {val}");
}
```

Every `for` loop you have written used an iterator underneath. Iterators let you write the "go through each element" logic once, in the standard library, instead of index variables in every loop.

All iterators implement one trait from the standard library:

```rust
pub trait Iterator {
    type Item;

    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<Self::Item>;

    // methods with default implementations elided
}
```

`next` returns `Some(item)` until the sequence is done, then `None`. That is the only method an implementor must write. Dozens of other methods (`map`, `filter`, `sum`...) have default implementations built on `next`.

